      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner needs to recall the impulse‑momentum relationship. I will ask a focused diagnostic question that prompts them to state the correct formula without giving the answer outright.",
      "content": "Which equation links the net force applied during a collision, the duration of the collision, and the change in momentum of trolley A?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I think it is F = \\Delta p / \\Delta t, but I'm not sure how to use it for this question."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The student has correctly identified the impulse‑momentum formula. I now confirm the implication: if the force increases while the momentum change stays the same, the collision time must decrease. I will give the final answer, restate the supporting equation, and add a concise formula breakdown and exam tip.",
      "content": "That is correct – the impulse‑momentum relationship is\n$$F_{\\text{net}}\\,\\Delta t = \\Delta p \\quad\\text{or}\\quad F_{\\text{net}} = \\frac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}.$$\nSince the change in momentum (\\(\\Delta p\\)) is unchanged and the net force (\\(F_{\\text{net}}\\)) is greater, the only way for the equation to stay balanced is for the collision time (\\(\\Delta t\\)) to become smaller.\n\n**Final Answer:** **DECREASES** (because \\(F_{\\text{net}}\\,\\Delta t = \\Delta p\\))\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** \\(F_{\\text{net}}\\,\\Delta t = \\Delta p\\)\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * \\(F_{\\text{net}}\\): net force acting on the object during the collision (in newtons, N)\n  * \\(\\Delta t\\): duration of the collision (in seconds, s)\n  * \\(\\Delta p\\): change in momentum of the object (in kilogram‑metres per second, kg·m/s)\n* **Why we use it:** This equation expresses the impulse (force multiplied by the time it acts) as being equal to the change in momentum. It lets us see how a larger force over a shorter time can produce the same momentum change as a smaller force over a longer time.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** When a question tells you that the momentum change stays the same but the force is larger, immediately think “time must get smaller”. Remember to keep units consistent – force in N, time in s, and momentum in kg·m/s.\n"
    }
